    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d285">

                <pb facs="0416" n="314" sameAs="frus:frus1927v03/pg314"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/4608</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the British
                        Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Howard</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-02-26"><hi rendition="#italic">February 26,
                        1927</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of Your Excellency’s note No. 141, of February 23, 1927, informing me
                    that His Majesty’s Government has reluctantly decided to send a man-of-war to
                    the west coast of Nicaragua, and that H. M. S. <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Colombo</hi> is being despatched to Corinto. I note that His Majesty’s
                    Government feels that the presence of a war vessel may have a moral effect and
                    will be a base of refuge for British subjects. I have further noted that it is
                    not <pb facs="0417" n="315" xml:id="pg315"/> intended to land forces and that
                    the commanding officer will be instructed accordingly.</p>
                <p>It gives me pleasure to repeat the assurances conveyed to Your Excellency in my
                    note of February 24, that the American armed forces now in Nicaragua will
                    continue to extend protection to British subjects in that country.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph C. Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
